# 10. Batch Design Processing

Implement a batch workflow for users working with multiple finished designs.

The user may have 10 to 30 completed designs ready for merchandise production.

Allow multiple images to be uploaded together.

Analyze each design and recommend the best product for it.

## Batch interface

Display a compact list or grid containing:

- Artwork thumbnail
- Design name or filename
- Recommended product
- Brief reason
- Alternative product selector
- Analysis status
- Copy Adaptation Prompt action

Allow the user to override any recommendation without reanalyzing the image.

Provide a clear overview of the product distribution across the batch.

For example:

- 5 apparel products
- 4 drinkware products
- 3 notebooks
- 4 posters
- 4 desk accessories

These figures are illustrative, not quotas.

Do not force an arbitrary product distribution.

## Batch intelligence

Evaluate each design individually.

When appropriate, consider the overall batch to avoid unnecessary repetition of the same product category.

However, design suitability must remain more important than variety.

If several designs clearly belong on T-shirts, recommend T-shirts.

## Batch API efficiency

- Process uploads with controlled concurrency.
- Respect the existing Gemini model fallback system.
- Avoid unnecessary duplicate analysis.
- Cache successful results.
- Show progress and failures clearly.
- Allow failed items to be retried individually.
- Do not restart a successful batch if one item fails.

Generate individual adaptation prompts locally using the stored analyses and product profiles whenever sufficient information exists.

Allow users to copy an individual prompt or export the batch recommendations and prompts together in a practical text or JSON format.

Do not implement automated Fourthwall product publishing in this version.

